The public officer shall have the authority needed to carry out the terms of this article, including:
(1) The right to investigate dwelling conditions in the city to determine which dwellings, buildings, structures or properties are unfit for human habitation, or current commercial, industrial or business use, or are vacant, dilapidated, being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes, or constitute a health hazard on private property;
(2) To administer oaths and affirmations, and to examine witnesses and receive evidence;
(3) To enter upon premises for the purpose of making examinations, so long as same are made in such a manner as to cause the least possible inconvenience to the persons in possession;
(4) To appoint and delegate the duties of such officers, agents and employees he deems necessary;
(5) To delegate any of his functions under this article to such officers and agents he deems necessary;
(6) The public officer may determine that a dwelling, building or structure is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial or business use if he finds that conditions exist in such dwelling, building or structure which are dangerous or injurious to the health, safety or morals of the occupants of same, or to the occupants of neighborhood dwellings, buildings or structures, or other residents of the county. Such conditions may include the following without limitation:
a. Defects therein increasing the hazards of fire, accidents, or other calamities;
b. Lack of adequate ventilation, light, or sanitary facilities;
c. Dilapidation;
d. Disrepair;
e. Structural defects;
f. Uncleanliness; and
g. Report of a law enforcement agency.
The public officer may determine, under existing ordinances, that a dwelling, building or structure is vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes upon a personal observation of report of a law enforcement agency and evidence of drug crimes being committed.
(Ord. No. 05-07, § 4, 9-11-07)

