(a) The following conditions being maintained or located on an owner's property may be declared to be nuisances when any one of them endangers the health, welfare, or good of other persons or the good order of the community:
(1) Stagnant water on premises;
(2) Any dead or decaying matter, weeds or grass on residential property exceeding 12 inches in height, vegetation, or any fruit, vegetable, animal, or rodent, upon premises which is odorous or capable of causing disease or annoyance to the inhabitants of the county;
a. This shall include, but not limited to, any growth of weeds, grass, or accumulation of waste or trash that may harbor insects, mosquitos, rodents, or snakes. Each area in violation may be deemed a separate and distinct nuisance on the property.
b. Trash shall be defined broadly as anything that is no longer readily useful or not usable in its existing form, anything broken beyond repair or otherwise worthless, including rubbish, refuse, garbage, litter, tires, discarded vegetation, and any other discarded or abandoned objects and accumulations.
(3) The generation of smoke or fumes in sufficient amounts to cause odor or annoyance to the inhabitants of the county;
(4) The pollution of public water or the injection of matter into the sewerage system which would be damaging thereto;
(5) Maintaining a dangerous or diseased animal or fowl;
(6) Obstruction of a public street highway, or sidewalk without a permit;
(7) Loud or unusual noises which are detrimental or annoying to the public, including, without limitation, unusual loud disturbances in or around churches or multiple-family complexes such as loud music and other activities in swimming pool and clubhouse areas;
(8) All walls, trees, and buildings that may endanger persons or property;
(9) Any business or building where illegal activities are habitually and commonly conducted in such a manner as to reasonably suggest that the operator of the business or building was aware of the illegal activities and failed to reasonably attempt to prevent the activities;
(10) Unused iceboxes, refrigerators, and the like, unless the doors, latches or locks thereof are removed; and
(11) Any other condition constituting a nuisance under state law or this Code.
(b) This section shall not be construed to be the exclusive definition of nuisance within this Code.
(Ord. No. 04-07, art. 3(§ 1), 9-11-07; Ord. No. O4-2024, § 1(Exh. A), 7-9-24)
